From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: wire up the crash reporter 0087 already built

0087 widened the console crash reporter in `osfmk/kern/exception.c` because the
MILANF link needed it: `bsd/conf/files` lists `bsd/dev/sweet_sample.c` as
`standard`, so it compiles on every board, and it calls `sweet_sample_task()`,
which lives in that reporter. Without the widen the link died on exactly one
symbol, `_sweet_sample_task`.

That patch did the minimum and said so: *"The caller in `bsd/kern/kern_exit.c`
carries its own guard and stays as it is."* The consequence is that on milanf the
kernel now links a full crash reporter - user registers, frame-pointer backtrace,
shared-cache slide, Mach-O header, heap strings - and then never calls it. Every
userspace crash would go unrecorded while the code to record it sat in the image.

The caller is `proc_prepareexit`, and the block is small:

```c
if (p == current_proc()) {
        extern void sweet_crash_log(const char *, int, int, uint64_t, uint64_t);
        sweet_crash_log(p->p_comm, proc_getpid(p), WTERMSIG(rv),
            ((uint64_t)ut->uu_exception << 32) | (uint32_t)ut->uu_code, ut->uu_subcode);
        /* abort_with_payload messages (_objc_fatal, os_crash_fmt, ...) */
        if (PROC_HAS_EXITREASON(p)) {
                char *desc = exit_reason_get_string_desc(p->p_exit_reason);
                printf("CRASH %s reason ns %u code 0x%llx: %s\n", ...);
        }
}
```

Two properties worth stating, because they are what make this safe to enable on
a board that is still bringing up:

*The `p == current_proc()` test is the filter.* It fires only when the process is
terminating *itself* - an actual crash - not when something else signals it. So
jetsam kills, `launchd`'s SIGKILL and the boot-time cleanup of failed jobs stay
out of the log, and what lands there is the crash you were going to have to
debug anyway. On a board with one console ring this is the difference between
noise and a signal.

*It costs nothing when nothing crashes.* The whole block is inside the exit path
of the exiting process; there is no hook, no timer, no per-syscall work.

This is the same reasoning 0096 recorded for `sweetiolog` and it is deliberately
complementary to it: `sweetiolog` says *which IOKit service a live daemon asked
for right before it gave up*, and this one says *where a dead one was when it
died*. Neither is a debugger, both are transcripts, and on a device with no
serial port and no way to get logs off it, transcripts are the whole toolkit.

Nothing in the block is board-specific: `PROC_HAS_EXITREASON`,
`exit_reason_get_string_desc`, `WTERMSIG` and `proc_getpid` are generic kernel
code, and `sweet_crash_log` itself has been compiled on milanf since 0087. The
`printf` lands in the console ring that 0089 added, which on milanf is the only
place it could land.

Verified without a compiler: fresh ci-tail (ci-art3 + 0086..0097), one gate
replaced, count asserted at 1, patch CR=0, source CR=0, sequential apply of
0086-0098 clean. Link unverified; CI in the morning.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_exit.c b/bsd/kern/kern_exit.c
index fde499c..bd3e22f 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_exit.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_exit.c
@@ -1884,7 +1884,7 @@ proc_prepareexit(proc_t p, int rv, boolean_t perf_notify)
 		subcode = ut->uu_subcode;
 		etype = ut->uu_exception;
 
-#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET) || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MILANF)
 		if (p == current_proc()) {
 			extern void sweet_crash_log(const char *, int, int, uint64_t, uint64_t);
 			sweet_crash_log(p->p_comm, proc_getpid(p), WTERMSIG(rv),
